 show that ${n}^{3}+2n=$ {{field-4}}, where $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ {{field-6}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}+2n\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\text{even}$ integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{3}+2n$ and show it is *even* by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=2b,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ $\\text{is}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is $\\text{even}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e35ca10a-b9bf-5e11-bbe4-4ebe568cbb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Schema"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae4d5412-a0d9-5ccf-856a-9f0c17094afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is one more than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422618} {:answer/id #uuid "d1824f54-11cc-5b07-bec5-bbd4ba448504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae4d5412-a0d9-5ccf-856a-9f0c17094afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is one more than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422618} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b3a1b37-5a5a-51bb-be8e-13678a7cce3c" :an