ince ${n}^{2}-2n$ {{field-6}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{2}-2n$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}-2n\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\text{odd}$ integer. Then, $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression ${n}^{2}-2n$ and show it is *odd* by writing it as one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a+1$ into the expression ${n}^{2}-2n$, we can show that ${n}^{2}-2n=2b+1,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{2}-2n$ $\\text{is one more than}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{2}-2n$ is $\\text{odd}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a1ebb1-37f3-5305-99b5-cf6ddc62e037" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Parity of a Nonlinear Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e33f66d1-bb0f-5f9b-b610-c8a6d2c6b464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376516} {:answer/id #uuid "27922783-4885-5f1f-8a8c-d505b39e4e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27922783-4885-5f1f-8a8c-d505b39e4e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376517} :answer-field/id #uuid "f451b86a-0266-5bde-ad24-f61785db8f02" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #e