ld/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "200a02a9-bb41-55a8-98b3-a6ee6658b9aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-184176" :question/problem "Prove that ${n}^{2}-2$ is odd provided that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}-2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-2={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}-2$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}-2\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression ${n}^{2}-2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}-2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =(2a+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-2 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+4a-1 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-2)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-1$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}-2=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}-2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is  one more than  a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}-2$ is odd."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a1ebb1-37f3-5305-99b5-cf6ddc62e037" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Parity of a Nonlinear Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9abac9a7-4bc1-5b4a-b0ef-25c359e9e66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2b+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422602} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3ca755-3710-5fe9-b102-89aa4ef7fa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2b-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422605} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d55c4e-7794-5f43-9ce0-4127ff3baf95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2a+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422622} {:answer/id #uuid "e83c0517-df33-56a4-abd5-74e112fc4d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422668} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5da349-2e57-5a40-b240-54ab6e23ba19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9abac9a7-4bc1-5b4a-b0ef-25c359e9e66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2b+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422602} :answer-field/id #uuid "07ac89ac-d480-5f09-9c89-2320aa157bdd" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422687} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029923eb-1887-521d-b691-06f7a68cb1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422493} {:a