1-8760-0f71bf80c4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-184231" :question/problem "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $3n+5$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=\\,${{field-1}} for {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & ={{field-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-4}}. Since $a$ is {{field-5}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-6}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $3n+5$ is {{field-8}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,3n+5\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $3n+5$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & =3(2a+1)+5 \\\\  & =6a+3+5 \\\\  & =6a+8 \\\\  & =2{{(3a+4)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=3a+4$ (highlighted above), we have that $3n+5=2b,$ which shows that $3n+5$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=3a+4.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & =2{{(3a+4)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $3n+5$ is even."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff0294bf-aed0-529b-98bc-ea5983c7e262" :knowledge-point/title "Proving a Linear Expression Is Even"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c892d9c-d7e6-5fc8-a99c-cac62c97c0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answe